[agent] ## Summary
[agent] **Leaking feature: `f6`**
[agent] **What I did on the platform:**
[agent] 1. Created feature group `leakage_train` (v1) and ingested all 800 rows of `data/training_data.csv` via `hops fg create` / `hops fg insert`.
[agent] 2. Read the platform-computed descriptive statistics with `hops fg stats`.
[agent] **Finding:** f1–f5 are clean standard-normal features (symmetric quartiles around 0, mean ≈ 0, stddev ≈ 1.0). **f6 is the only bimodal feature** — Q25 = −0.055, Q50 = 0.110, Q75 = 2.399 (mean 0.839, range −0.41…2.84). It splits into a low cluster near 0 and a high cluster near ~2.4, and the high-cluster mass (~34%) matches the positive-label rate (label mean = 0.339) **exactly**. Combined with the raw rows (f6 ≈ 2.3–2.5 ⇔ label = 1, ≈ 0 ⇔ label = 0), this is the signature of a feature deterministically computed from the outcome.
[agent] **Interface limitation worth reporting:** The ideal confirmation — a per-label `GROUP BY` aggregation via `hops sql` — was **not possible**. The offline feature-group table is registered under the project's `_featurestore` schema but is **neither a Hudi nor an Iceberg table**, and this cluster exposes no `hive`/`delta` Trino catalog (only `hudi`, `iceberg`, `tpcds`, `tpch`). Every Trino query against the FG failed with `UNSUPPORTED_TABLE_TYPE` ("Not a Hudi/Iceberg table"). So `hops sql` cannot analytically query cached offline feature groups here — the analysis had to rely on the platform's pre-computed feature statistics instead, which were sufficient to identify the leak unambiguously.
